1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,725
My name’s Liz Fuller and I’m a running coach with Compton Park Runners

2
00:00:04,725 --> 00:00:05,040
Club.

3
00:00:05,040 --> 00:00:06,769
Welcome to my podcast.

4
00:00:06,769 --> 00:00:11,070
If you’re thinking about taking up running – I’m here to help.

5
00:00:11,070 --> 00:00:17,121
There are many training programmes available online which aim to help people build up

6
00:00:17,121 --> 00:00:18,850
to running 5 kilometres.

7
00:00:18,850 --> 00:00:24,619
Some of them are great and thousands of people of all ages are taking

8
00:00:24,619 --> 00:00:28,740
part in 5-kilometre races across the country as a result.

9
00:00:28,740 --> 00:00:33,821
People like them because they’re easy to follow and don’t push them too

10
00:00:33,821 --> 00:00:34,160
hard.

11
00:00:34,160 --> 00:00:40,900
However, they don’t work for everyone – especially if you suffer from something like a heart condition

12
00:00:40,900 --> 00:00:41,693
or asthma,

13
00:00:41,693 --> 00:00:46,450
because they’re aimed at people with average fitness and running ability.

14
00:00:46,450 --> 00:00:51,680
Another thing is that everyone is different – and if you have any specific

15
00:00:51,680 --> 00:00:56,910
questions related to your needs, there’s no one to provide any answers.

16
00:00:56,910 --> 00:01:02,260
I have a couple of simple tips I always give to new runners.

17
00:01:02,260 --> 00:01:08,527
I expect you’ve been told to run very slowly until your fitness increases –

18
00:01:08,527 --> 00:01:11,660
well, I find that can prevent progress.

19
00:01:11,660 --> 00:01:15,256
You should run at a speed that feels comfortable,

20
00:01:15,256 --> 00:01:20,050
but time yourself and try to run a bit faster each time.

21
00:01:20,050 --> 00:01:24,879
Listening to music can be very helpful – it takes your mind off things

22
00:01:24,879 --> 00:01:27,850
and helps your body get into a rhythm.

23
00:01:27,850 --> 00:01:33,489
I’d say that is better than running with a friend – especially as most people are competitive

24
00:01:33,489 --> 00:01:37,470
and that’s not what you want when you’re just starting.

25
00:01:37,470 --> 00:01:42,563
I don’t think the time of day is especially important – some people are

26
00:01:42,563 --> 00:01:44,019
better in the evening,

27
00:01:44,019 --> 00:01:48,020
while others are morning people – but you need to be consistent,

28
00:01:48,020 --> 00:01:52,750
so aim to train regularly – twice a week is enough to begin with.

29
00:01:52,750 --> 00:01:57,655
New members often say to me that they’ve been put off running either

30
00:01:57,655 --> 00:01:59,056
because they lack confidence,

31
00:01:59,056 --> 00:02:01,158
or they don’t have time,

32
00:02:01,158 --> 00:02:03,260
or they think they dislike running.

33
00:02:03,260 --> 00:02:08,250
Ceri, for example, joined the club two years ago at the age of 40.

34
00:02:08,250 --> 00:02:13,269
She’d always enjoyed running at school but wasn’t sure if she’d be able to

35
00:02:13,269 --> 00:02:13,860
do it.

36
00:02:13,860 --> 00:02:18,580
She was worried about being left behind and being the slowest runner.

37
00:02:18,580 --> 00:02:23,699
But she says she was made to feel so welcome she soon forgot all

38
00:02:23,699 --> 00:02:24,430
about that.

39
00:02:24,430 --> 00:02:29,357
James had always hated the idea of running but a friend encouraged him to

40
00:02:29,357 --> 00:02:33,580
come along for a taster session and he hasn’t looked back.

41
00:02:33,580 --> 00:02:39,460
He never misses a training session despite having a really demanding job.

42
00:02:39,460 --> 00:02:44,256
Leo was worried about having to commit himself to training sessions every week

43
00:02:44,256 --> 00:02:49,421
and wasn’t sure he’d be able to fit training into his busy

44
00:02:49,421 --> 00:02:49,790
schedule.

45
00:02:49,790 --> 00:02:55,213
But after experiencing a lot of stress at work he came along to us

46
00:02:55,213 --> 00:02:57,150
and gave it a go.

47
00:02:57,150 --> 00:03:02,680
Now he says he feels much more relaxed and he looks forward to his

48
00:03:02,680 --> 00:03:03,470
weekly run.

49
00:03:03,470 --> 00:03:07,400
Mark is quite typical of our new members.

50
00:03:07,400 --> 00:03:12,544
He’s never considered himself to be a sporty person and it was only when

51
00:03:12,544 --> 00:03:17,344
he retired that he decided to take up the challenge of trying to run

52
00:03:17,344 --> 00:03:18,030
5 kilometres.

53
00:03:18,030 --> 00:03:22,393
It took him months to find the courage to contact us

54
00:03:22,393 --> 00:03:27,947
but felt reassured immediately as there were other people his age who were only

55
00:03:27,947 --> 00:03:31,120
just taking up running for the first time.

56
00:03:31,120 --> 00:03:34,217
My own journey hasn’t been easy.

57
00:03:34,217 --> 00:03:40,410
I did my first marathon when I was 37, after having had two kids.

58
00:03:40,410 --> 00:03:43,185
My husband had been running marathons for years,

59
00:03:43,185 --> 00:03:47,000
but I never dreamed I’d be doing one with him.

60
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:50,134
I managed to complete it in four hours,

61
00:03:50,134 --> 00:03:55,228
but I felt like giving up halfway through – it was only the support

62
00:03:55,228 --> 00:03:57,970
of the spectators that kept me going.

63
00:03:57,970 --> 00:04:04,053
I do think signing up for a race of whatever length is motivating – whether it’s 5K

64
00:04:04,053 --> 00:04:08,347
or 25K – because it’s good to have something to work towards

65
00:04:08,347 --> 00:04:11,210
and it gives you a sense of achievement.

66
00:04:11,210 --> 00:04:15,020
I did my first 10K after only six months,

67
00:04:15,020 --> 00:04:20,100
which was certainly very challenging and not something I’d necessarily recommend.

68
00:04:20,100 --> 00:04:24,772
But after you’ve been training for a few weeks, it’s worth putting your name

69
00:04:24,772 --> 00:04:25,940
down for a 5K.

70
00:04:25,940 --> 00:04:30,373
Some people find they only need a few practice runs before taking part in

71
00:04:30,373 --> 00:04:31,007
a race,

72
00:04:31,007 --> 00:04:34,490
but I’d give yourself a couple of months at least.

73
00:04:34,490 --> 00:04:37,850
Well, I hope that’s given you a really good idea .

74
00:04:37,850 --> 00:04:37,900
.

75
00:04:37,900 --> 00:04:37,950
.
